torId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#36568004330", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Remigius de Fécamp was an 11th-century religious leader. He was a Benedictine monk who was a supporter of William the Conqueror and was appointed Bishop of Dorchester and Bishop of Lincoln.", "newValue": "Remigius de Fécamp, a prominent Benedictine monk and ecclesiastical leader, played a pivotal role in the Normanization of the English Church following the Norman Conquest of 1066. Born around 1050, likely in Normandy, he served as almoner of Fécamp Abbey before becoming a staunch supporter of William the Conqueror. His career trajectory exemplifies the deliberate policy of replacing Anglo-Saxon clergy with Norman loyalists, a strategic move by William to consolidate political and religious control over his new kingdom.

Initially appointed Bishop of Dorchester in 1067, Remigius rapidly ascended the ecclesiastical hierarchy. He participated in William's coronation and was instrumental in reorganizing the English diocesan structure. In 1072, during the Council of Winchester, his see was transferred from the ancient, declining Dorchester to the strategically important and growing city of Lincoln. This move, sanctioned by William and Archbishop Lanfranc, initiated the construction of Lincoln Cathedral, a monumental undertaking that symbolized the new Norman order and established Lincoln as one of England's most significant bishoprics.

